dunitdunit‚dmarkf200000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;qGdTCCNKHE3T8FTvt9hi78RUor0bB8CGYrRhK3taTfcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarks0.14274539999999997ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;p6Vp6-J5I0hM8QYRBXW0EVmyKzXxvPidzjuxjunZlk0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka3ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;C28soPU0j9O3qR4PfsI9gKfAhpiWSMDPEwlfWvyNSqMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarke32000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;4DdZxiSylA4KE0MVLapHzcnKkV6wKGlcdcBy0Dbhk34ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkd4500ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Iob-hCg7qCJdHfCVLsAzx4MYgrYx2wiUl6H_JoHoDGgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;6od80hyD77_8BO9aGWcli32D_A7yYb6WoHmP3He13sEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;WC5SgSNpzuLlzn5PNE5uYCVLaUsZaPobQ_HcT0L9hcsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Y1OtWGXZGM7KB3WwpHkVaRLef9G2fy-xTNYPwRQikB8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky­A participatory art initiative centered on a decades-old sculpture is working to expand public memory of Alex Odeh, a Palestinian-American civil rights activist assassinated in California forty years ago, according to [Hyperallergic](https://hyperallergic.com/a-living-memorial-to-palestinian-activist-alex-odeh-takes-flight/).

Odeh was born in 1944 in Jifna, Palestine, into a Catholic family, according to Hyperallergic. As a child he experienced the displacement of the Nakba, and later witnessed the Israeli military occupation of the West Bank following 1967. After immigrating to the United States, he completed a Master's degree in political science at California State University, Fullerton, in 1972, and went on to teach Arabic language and Middle Eastern history. By the early 1980s he had become a West Coast regional director of the American-Arab Anti-Discrimination Committee (ADC), an organization dedicated to challenging anti-Arab discrimination, hate crimes, and harmful stereotypes, according to Hyperallergic. His work included fostering dialogue across Jewish, Muslim, and Christian communities, and he was scheduled to address a gathering at a local synagogue the day following Friday prayer services when he was killed, the outlet reports.

On October 11, 1985, a pipe bomb tore through the ADC's Santa Ana offices, killing Odeh, according to Hyperallergic. His murder has never been officially resolved, and no one has been arrested. The outlet reports that investigative journalists and the FBI identified members of a right-wing Jewish Defense League as possibly responsible, though the case remains open.

A bronze statue depicting Odeh holding a dove â€” created in 1994 by Algerian-American artist Khalil Bendib as an image of peace â€” stands outside the Santa Ana public library, according to Hyperallergic. The memorial has been repeatedly targeted: red paint was thrown on it in 1996 and again in 1997, the bronze dove was stolen, eventually recovered, and reattached in 2020, and brick remnants from a more recent act of vandalism reportedly lay scattered around the sculpture. Last December, the dove was removed from the statue with the stated aim of keeping Odeh's legacy active rather than inert.

That removal set the stage for a new project. Artist Jon Rubin first encountered the Odeh statue during a 2023 residency at the Grand Central Art Center (GCAC) in Santa Ana, according to Hyperallergic. Rubin told the outlet he stayed because of a combination of factors, and was struck by how many people he met â€” including a librarian â€” had little familiarity with Odeh or with the sustained hostility the memorial has faced. Many, he found, did not know that Odeh's work had been rooted in coalition-building rather than confrontation.

The initiative that resulted reimagines the memorial's form entirely. Rather than a permanent fixture anchored to a single public square, Rubin described the concept to Hyperallergic as a decentralized, participatory approach: the dove travels among various hosts, sparking gatherings built around Odeh's values of hospitality and cross-community conversation. Over several months, according to Hyperallergic, the piece will move through a range of individuals, organizations, dinners, and performances.

